WEBVTT

00:00:01.710 --> 00:00:02.755
Hi, I'm Keel!

00:00:02.755 --> 00:00:06.237
In 2009 Satoshi shipped a Windows program with its source.

00:00:06.237 --> 00:00:09.022
This year someone rebuilt it, byte for byte.

00:00:10.642 --> 00:00:12.536
One wrong byte gives a different hash.

00:00:12.536 --> 00:00:15.783
So the compiler, the linkers, the libraries, the shell and even the

00:00:15.783 --> 00:00:18.759
clock had to be read back out of the old file.

00:00:21.592 --> 00:00:22.760
First the libraries.

00:00:22.760 --> 00:00:27.430
wxWidgets and Berkeley DB were built in November, with an older binutils.

00:00:27.850 --> 00:00:29.313
Then binutils was upgraded.

00:00:29.313 --> 00:00:33.703
Bitcoin's own files were compiled as a debug build, with full optimisation

00:00:33.703 --> 00:00:35.532
only on the hashing file.

00:00:35.952 --> 00:00:39.658
Windres packed the icons and the manifest, and stamped every resource folder

00:00:39.658 --> 00:00:41.202
with the time it ran.

00:00:41.622 --> 00:00:45.381
Last, strip removed the symbols and wrote its own time into the header.

00:00:45.381 --> 00:00:46.827
That file is what shipped.

00:00:48.292 --> 00:00:50.660
The header says GNU ld, not Microsoft's linker.

00:00:50.660 --> 00:00:52.733
There is no Rich header at all.

00:00:53.153 --> 00:00:56.020
In the code, import stubs sit eight bytes apart.

00:00:56.020 --> 00:00:58.569
The repository ties that to the newer binutils.

00:00:58.989 --> 00:01:02.903
The strings name gcc three point four point five, and keep include

00:01:02.903 --> 00:01:06.164
paths that only a plain command window would leave alone.

00:01:06.584 --> 00:01:11.495
The resources carry seventy nine time stamps, and a manifest with Windows line endings.

00:01:12.758 --> 00:01:16.977
335 bytes differ if the three clock readings are left real.

00:01:21.050 --> 00:01:24.864
The target is the hash of the released file, six point four megabytes.

00:01:24.864 --> 00:01:27.504
The rebuild prints match only when it is reached.

00:01:28.892 --> 00:01:33.614
A build that pins its toolchain and fixes its clock is reproducible by design.

00:01:33.614 --> 00:01:35.975
This one had to be reproduced backwards.

00:01:38.025 --> 00:01:39.156
Source, tools and clock.

00:01:39.156 --> 00:01:41.136
Get all three, and the hash follows.

00:01:42.625 --> 00:01:44.691
Every source is in the full article.

00:01:44.691 --> 00:01:45.281
I'm Keel.

00:01:45.281 --> 00:01:45.576
Bye!
